          "title": "Appendix A. Detailed Feature Extraction Methods",
          "textSnippet": "Appendix A. Detailed Feature Extraction Methods To construct the kinematic metrics in the endpoint space, the endpoint displacementbetween two frames is calculated first:whereis the Cartesian 3D coordinates of the wrist in theth frame andis the total number of recorded frames.multiplies fps is the velocity and the sum is the path length. The computable joint angles involved in the moving process include: shoulder flexion/extension angle (), adduction/abduction angle (), internal/external rotation angle () and elbow flexion/extension angle (). The first three angles were defined by Euler angles from the rotation matrix with XZY sequence, which is further elaborated below. The coordinate systems of the thorax and the humerus segments for both sides from the 18-joint skeleton of RealSense is shown in. Take the right side as an example, and the left side is similar. The definition of the thorax segment coordinate systemis as follows: SpineMid (SM) is defined as the midpoint of HR and HL (). The-axis is the unit vector going from SM to SS (), the-axis is the unit vector perpendicular to the the plane formed by-axis and the vector from SR to SM (), and the-axis is defined byand-axes to c..."
